\subsection{\code{Ggplot2RocLayerProcessor$attach_thresholds()}}{
  Attach each point's decision threshold, when the layer
carries one

A \code{threshold} column survives the build only for a \code{maidr_roc()}
layer, whose geom names the aesthetic. The line processor drops the
rows whose y is \code{NA} and splits the rest by group in the group's own
order, so the same filter and split here put the thresholds back
beside the points they were scored at. A series whose count disagrees
-- a row the line processor dropped for a reason other than \code{NA} y --
gets no thresholds rather than the wrong ones.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{Ggplot2RocLayerProcessor$attach_thresholds(data, built, panel_id = NULL)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{data}}{The series list}
      \item{\code{built}}{Built plot data}
      \item{\code{panel_id}}{Panel ID for faceted plots (optional)}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    The series list, with \code{threshold} on each point that has one
  }
}

